britain's secret intelligence service  mi6 has always been shrouded in mystery  for decades the chief was only known by  the letter c  and until 1994 the secret service  officially didn't exist  but mi6 is stepping out of the shadows  to stay secret we're going to have to  become more open  richard moore the current chief of mi6  is the first c to publicly use social  media  in one of his first ever interviews he  speaks to the economist asks podcast  about the biggest threats facing the  world today  from a possible russian invasion of  ukraine to china's growing capability to  access private data around the world  richard moore welcome to the economist  asks well it's very nice to be here  thank you for inviting me also in the  studio is shashank joshi he's the  economist defense editor shashank good  to have you here too it's great to be  here thanks what we've been watching  most closely in the last couple of weeks  has been this staggering military build  up on the edges of ukraine and watching  with growing concern i think there's  there's very important questions as to  russian intentions what is putin up to  uh very simply will he invade i think  vladimir putin does think in terms of  spheres of influence i mean very clearly  and he articulated that in his in his  public language  um he he really does think that russia  in the 21st century has the right to  sort of impose  limits on the sovereignty of the of the  countries on its periphery and and  that's a problem because uh those  countries are not going to accept that  and of course you know from our  perspective the uk and other and our  allies neither are we i mean you can't  um say that's fine uh mr putin  you get to choose what the people of  ukraine  get to decide you know to do with their  with their state it's not  it's just not possible so we do have  this sort of rather chronic clash of  ideas and ideology around this issue  it's really important that we're we're  very clear in our communicating uh to mr  putin about the you know the price that  would be paid if he were to push ahead  with that i mean i i hope he won't i  think  uh the ukraine has moved on since  uh 2014 i think they would  they are more able um to resist  um and um clearly the sort of economic  and financial price would be huge for  you know for a russian people that's  already suffering from a raft of  sanctions from 2014. let's turn to the  big challenges that you addressed in a  speech  in london recently and you said in the  first speech you've given us head of mi6  indeed that the greatest single priority  for the service is to adapt to the rise  of china it seems like quite a zigzag  from a few years ago when the messaging  seemed to be china is a great trading  investment partner and that we should be  open to china again what has changed or  do have you just reached a different  judgment  it goes back to why i gave a speech i i  wanted to explain uh a bit about what  the service is doing and uh the fact is  that um  the work we're doing on on china is now  the most important work that we are  doing and it is it's not that the work  we do on russia and  international terrorism is is any less  important than it was it's just that the  prominence and the need to understand  china  has grown because  china's influence and place in the world  has grown  but i said very clearly in the speech  that i thought we needed um to have a  positive agenda with china that we  needed to trade with china and that we  needed uh their cooperation on the key  transnational issues climate change uh  biodiversity at the end of the day  climate change is the biggest issue of  all  so you know i was advocating the need to  cooperate with china on that but at the  same time i was very clear that where um  china  because it is  a government which is an authoritarian  government with very different values  from our own where  it pursues its interests and its value  systems in ways which clash with our own  it's quite important that we are robust  in standing up for those values and  interests and in particular  as the integrated review set out  the key  sort of battleground if you like is  around technology do we  compete effectively with the chinese in  the really big technologies of the 21st  century  i think what's really interesting about  that is when we think of russia as an  intelligence challenge we think of  novichok in salisbury we think of  subversion across europe sort of  paramilitary um destruction  when we think of china it's very  different but our understanding is still  evolving you know i think people  instinctively understand the debate over  huawei that is control of  telecommunications gear can give a  foreign country a foreign agency access  to data i think what we're yet to fully  understand or debate  is things that seem less directly  involved in the security space that is  chinese access to data around ride  sharing apps dating apps genetic testing  kits biometric data  i think you hinted at some of this in  your speech richard talking about a  global surveillance web and how it  affects the states that are buying this  stuff from the chinese but also how it  affects your agents and officers and  their ability to go about their work the  data trap that i was trying to  articulate is much more difficult to  define isn't it  and but it is the issue that we had the  great debate around huawei in this  country it's just that overseas i think  that that level of understanding of  these issues is not as high as it is  here and it and it and it needs to be  because over time if you allow access  into key data  then then you will slowly erode your  sovereignty and of course  china china is looking for this  these data streams because  the greater amount of data you get but  also critically the amount of diversity  of data you get from around the globe  the better your ability to build the  artificial intelligence systems of the  future  thanks for watching you can listen to  the full interview on our podcast the  economist asks click the link to find  the episode and don't forget to  subscribe
 

